  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "batch",
   "id": "b035",
   "spec": "Wrap fit_predict's final test-time fit in a bagging loop over scaffold groups. Build the Murcko scaffold group array as now, then for each of five repeats with rng = np.random.default_rng(seed + repeat), sample the unique scaffold groups with replacement, take all rows belonging to the sampled groups as the bag, call base_predict on that bag to predict the test molecules, apply the already-fitted meta-learner weights, and store the result. Return the median of the five resulting prediction vectors. To keep inside the time limit, reduce the inner out-of-fold stacking loop that produces the meta-learner's training matrix from five folds to three. Sampling whole scaffolds rather than rows keeps the resampling aligned with how the harness splits data.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Scaffold-group bagged ensemble of the whole stack"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "data",
   "id": "b041",
   "spec": "Compute the generic Murcko scaffold (MakeScaffoldGeneric) for each molecule. Inside base_predict, for the tree learners only, add one column: the out-of-fold smoothed mean of y for the molecule's generic scaffold. Use smoothing m=5 toward the global mean. For training rows, compute it by leave-one-group-out using the Murcko scaffold groups ga. For test rows, use the full-training statistics. Unseen scaffolds get the global mean. Pass the generic scaffold strings through as an extra argument.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Generic-scaffold target-encoding feature"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "other",
   "id": "b047",
   "spec": "Add one round of self-training at the end of fit_predict. After computing the test predictions in the usual way, form an augmented training set consisting of the real training rows plus the test rows carrying their predicted values as pseudo-labels, with sample weights 1.0 for real rows and 0.3 for pseudo-labelled rows. Refit only the LightGBM and random-forest base learners on this augmented set (pass the weights through sample_weight and LightGBM's weight argument), leaving the kernel member and the meta-learner weights from the first pass unchanged, and recombine with the same meta-learner coefficients to give the final predictions. Guard the whole second pass in a try/except that returns the first-pass predictions on any failure, and keep the extra fit cheap enough that total runtime stays well under ten minutes.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Transductive pseudo-label self-training round"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "optimizer",
   "id": "b053",
   "spec": "The stack's meta-learner is a Ridge with positive=True and alpha=1e-3, which minimises squared error even though the harness scores mean absolute error. Replace it with a direct L1 fit: solve min over w >= 0 and intercept b of sum_i |y_i - (b + w . oof_i)| using scipy.optimize.linprog with the standard epigraph formulation (one slack variable per training row, two inequality constraints per row, bounds that keep the weights non-negative and the intercept free, method='highs'). Apply the fitted weights and intercept to the base-learner predictions on the test set. If linprog fails to converge, fall back to the existing Ridge. The number of base columns is three, so the program is small and fast.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Non-negative L1 meta-learner via linear programming"
  },
  {
   "alpha": 1,
   "beta": 2,
   "family": "schedule",
   "id": "b059",
   "spec": "In the stack's LightGBM member, train 900 rounds with a learning rate that decays from 0.06 to 0.01 along a cosine curve. Implement it with lgb.reset_parameter(learning_rate=lambda i: ...) passed as a callback. Keep the other parameters unchanged.",
   "status": "done",
   "times_tried": 1,
   "title": "Cosine-decayed learning rate for LightGBM"
  }
 ],
